Build Your Own Rockbox

1. Clone 'rockbox' from git (or extract a downloaded archive).

   $ git clone git://git.rockbox.org/rockbox

     or

   $ tar xJf rockbox.tar.xz

2. Create a build directory, preferably in the same directory as the firmware/
   and apps/ directories. This is where all generated files will be written.

   $ cd rockbox
   $ mkdir build
   $ cd build

3. Make sure you have mips/m68k/arm-elf-gcc and siblings in the PATH. Make sure
   that you have 'perl' in your PATH too. Your gcc cross compiler needs to be
   a particular version depending on what player you are compiling for. These
   can be generated using the rockboxdev.sh script in the /tools/ folder of the
   source.

   $ which arm-elf-eabi-gcc
   $ which perl

4. In your build directory, run the 'tools/configure' script and enter what
   target you want to build for and if you want a debug version or not (and a
   few more questions). It'll prompt you. The debug version is for making a
   gdb version out of it. It is only useful if you run gdb towards your target
   Archos.

   $ ../tools/configure

5. *ploink*. Now you have got a Makefile generated for you.

6. Run 'make' and soon the necessary pieces from the firmware and the apps
   directories have been compiled, linked and scrambled for you.

   $ make
   $ make zip

7. unzip the rockbox.zip on your music player, reboot it and
   *smile*.

If you want to build for more than one target, just create several build
directories and create a setup for each target:

   $ mkdir build-fuzeplus
   $ cd build-fuzeplus
   $ ../tools/configure

   $ mkdir build-xduoox3
   $ cd build-xduoox3
   $ ../tools/configure

Build The Android Simulator

The iPod Video, iPod mini 2G, Sansa Clip Zip and Toshiba Gigabeat F/X
simulators also run as an Android app, with the buttons and clickwheel
working by touch; Settings > Android > Model switches between them. See
uisimulator/android/README for the details.

1. Install the Android SDK with build-tools, platforms;android-35 and an NDK
   (r27 or later), for example with Android Studio's SDK Manager or with
   sdkmanager. You also need a JDK, CMake and curl.

   $ sdkmanager "build-tools;35.0.0" "platforms;android-35" "ndk;28.2.13676358"

2. configure finds the SDK in ~/Android/Sdk (or $ANDROID_HOME) and uses its
   newest NDK. If yours are elsewhere, tell it where:

   $ export ANDROID_SDK_PATH=~/Android/Sdk
   $ export ANDROID_NDK_PATH=~/Android/Sdk/ndk/<version>

3. Create a build directory and pick target 202, "Android iPod sim". The first
   time, configure downloads SDL2 and builds it into the build directory.

   $ mkdir build-android
   $ cd build-android
   $ ../tools/configure

4. Build the simulator and its files, then package them as an APK. This
   also builds the other models, in models/ in the build directory.

   $ make
   $ make install
   $ ../uisimulator/android/build-apk.sh

5. Install rockbox-sim.apk on your phone, allow it access to all files when
   it asks, and *smile*. Your phone's storage is the player's disk, and
   Rockbox keeps each model's own files in /sdcard/Rockbox/<model>/.rockbox.

   $ adb install -r rockbox-sim.apk

Questions anyone? Ask on the mailing list or on IRC. We'll be happy to help you!
